    slug: "odoo-16-to-19-upgrade-downtime",
    title: "Planning downtime for an Odoo 16 → 19 upgrade",
    description: "Odoo 16 left standard support in September 2025, and Odoo 17 in September 2026 (Odoo's supported-versions page).",
    body: "Odoo 16 left standard support in September 2025, and Odoo 17 in September 2026 (Odoo's supported-versions page). If your test upgrade took hours, not minutes, here is how to plan the production cut-over without guessing.\n\n**1. The time is processing, not queue.** Odoo staff confirmed on the forum that the upgrade queue wait is under 10 minutes and can't be booked. A long test upgrade means your database takes that long to convert.\n\n**2. Rehearse, and time each step.** Odoo's upgrade documentation recommends requesting fresh test upgrades regularly, \"especially if it takes a long time to finish\", and a full rehearsal the day before go-live. Plan the production window as if it takes as long as your last test, plus margin.\n\n**3. Find out what is slow.** For Odoo-hosted databases, open a ticket under \"An issue related to my future upgrade (I am testing an upgrade)\" and ask which steps took longest. On Odoo.sh the test upgrade log is in `~/logs/upgrade.log`. If the time goes into a custom module's computed stored fields on a large table, Odoo's custom-upgrade guide says an upgrade script can fill them with SQL to cut processing time.\n\n**4. Downtime starts at upload.** Anything entered after the database copy is sent is not in the upgraded database. Stop users, scheduled imports and integrations at that point.\n\n**5. Count the work after the upgrade.** On-premise, the upgraded database comes back without a filestore; merge it with your production filestore before going live. Then run a short smoke test: one sale through delivery and invoice, one purchase receipt, a payment, and each integration. On Odoo Online, a completed production upgrade can't be reverted, so decide your go/no-go point before you start.\n\n**6. Custom modules decide the timeline.** Odoo's upgrade service converts standard apps and Studio customisations. Modules built in-house or by a partner aren't covered unless they're under a maintenance-of-customisations subscription. The database can't be upgraded until each custom module has a version for the target release.",
    sources: "Sources: odoo.com/documentation/19.0/administration/upgrade.html and /developer/howtos/upgrade_custom_db.html (read 17 Nov 2026); Odoo supported-versions page (read 16 Nov 2026); Odoo forum thread 370937.",
    closing: "Planning an upgrade with custom modules? We'll review your module list and give a written fixed-price plan, free.",
