    description: "Odoo 16 has been out of standard support since September 2025.",
    body: "Odoo 16 has been out of standard support since September 2025. Odoo 19 is supported until September 2028, and Odoo 20 until September 2029 (Odoo's supported-versions page). In November 2026, Odoo staff said on the forum that upgrades to 20 weren't available yet and were planned before year end. So for a customised Odoo 16, the real choice is: go to 19 now, or wait.\n\n**What decides it is your custom code, not Odoo.** Odoo's upgrade service converts the standard apps and Studio customisations. Modules developed in-house or by a partner are not included unless they're under a maintenance-of-customisations subscription. And the database can't be upgraded until every custom module has a version for the target release.\n\n**Four steps before you choose:**\n1. **List every installed module** as standard, third-party or custom. For each custom one, write down what it does, and check whether the target version now does it in standard. Odoo's own guide recommends freezing development and challenging each customisation before porting.\n2. **Request an upgraded test database.** With an Enterprise subscription, upgrades are free, and you can request several test databases. It shows you what breaks while you decide.\n3. **Write a test script from your real flows.** In multi-company setups that means a purchase in one company creating the sale in the other, the stock moves on both sides, and the invoices and their reconciliation. Run it on the test database before trusting any version.\n4. **Decide by the gap.** Odoo's docs note that the smaller the version gap, the easier the upgrade. Waiting for 20 means one bigger jump, 16 → 20. Going to 19 now means a smaller jump today and another smaller one later.",
    sources: "Sources: odoo.com/documentation/19.0/administration/upgrade.html (upgrade SLA, custom-module warning), /developer/howtos/upgrade_custom_db.html, read 17 Nov 2026; Odoo supported-versions page, read 16 Nov 2026; Odoo forum thread 374971.",
    closing: "Not sure how big your custom-module port is? Send us your module list for a free review and a written fixed price.",
