    description: "A small supplier on the Odoo forum was doing forecasting and reordering by hand in Odoo and Google Sheets, and found add-on tools too expensive.",
    body: "A small supplier on the Odoo forum was doing forecasting and reordering by hand in Odoo and Google Sheets, and found add-on tools too expensive. Odoo 19 already covers most of it. What it doesn't do is statistical forecasting.\n\n1. **Reordering rules (min/max) per product.** Odoo compares the forecasted quantity (on hand + incoming − outgoing) with your minimum, and orders up to your maximum.\n2. **Inventory > Operations > Procurement > Replenishment** lists the products below their minimum, with a suggested quantity and an Order button. With the rule's trigger on Auto, the daily scheduled action \"Procurement: run scheduler\" creates the RFQs. With Manual, you review and click Order.\n3. **Vendor lead times** on each product decide how early Odoo orders. The Replenishment Horizon setting (365 days by default) decides how far ahead it looks.\n4. **Forecasting, done cheaply:** reordering rules don't compute min/max from sales history. Once a month, export 6–12 months of sales per product to a spreadsheet, set min = average daily sales × (lead time + safety days) and max = min + one order cycle, and import the new values into the rules. The spreadsheet does the thinking; Odoo does the ordering.\n\nStart with your 20 best sellers on a duplicate database. Compare a week of suggestions with what you'd have ordered by hand, then switch those to Auto.",
    sources: "Sources: Odoo 19.0 source: `stock.warehouse.orderpoint` trigger Auto/Manual, Replenishment menu and Order button, \"Procurement: run scheduler\" cron (daily), Replenishment Horizon default 365, read 16 Nov 2026; Odoo forum thread 391776.",
    closing: "Questions about your own Odoo setup? Ask for a free review and a written fixed price.",
